  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/ageneiosus_polystictus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Family: Auchenipteridae. Size: 45 cm. Temperature: 22–24°C. pH: 6.5–7.5. Common name: none.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/ageneiosus_polystictus.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Brazil, Negro and lower Branco River basins. Type locality: mouth of the Rio Negro.</p><p>Ageneiosus polystictus is endemic to Brazil, described for the Rio Negro, with occurrences in other blackwater drainages such as the Uatumã, Jaú, Nhamundá (Amazonas state) and Juruti (Pará state) rivers, all in the middle Amazon. It is a widely distributed species in these basins. No threats have been detected that could endanger its population; therefore, Ageneiosus polystictus has been categorized as Least Concern (IUCN 2022).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/ageneiosus_polystictus.htm",
      "html": "<p>A large tank would be needed with plenty of swimming space.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/ageneiosus_polystictus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Meat based.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/ageneiosus_polystictus.htm",
      "html": "<p>As with other fish of this genus, they will be fine as juveniles, but as they mature they become predatory so would need to be kept with fish that are not too small.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/ageneiosus_polystictus.htm",
      "html": "<p>This genus was previously placed in its own family, Ageneiosidae, and some publications still follow this line.</p><p>Etymology: the genus name Ageneiosus means 'beardless', referring to the very small maxillary barbels. The specific name polystictus is from the Greek poly = 'many' and stiktos = 'spotted', alluding to the small spots on this species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/auchenipteridae/ageneiosus_polystictus.htm",
